 - Always improve **bonuses first**, prioritizing **food** bonuses. Major refactor of `CvUnitAI::AI_bestCityBuild` to use a probabilistic value tree tuned for AdvCiv-SAS. See [KI#30](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#30---attemptingly-fixed-ai-workers-not-prioritizing-bonuses-to-improve-especially-food-bonuses-and-other-ai-workers-optimizations-orand-changes).
 - One worker per improvement **cap** to avoid over-stacking on slow tiles and reduce capture risk.
 - **Reliable Worker availability without early overproduction:** AI cities rebuild enough Workers for each land area before other production priorities can leave them short indefinitely, without producing more than the AI calculates it needs. Maintaining enough Workers throughout the game lets the AI improve food/production/commerce, connect bonuses and routes, repair pillaging, and develop new or underdeveloped cities sooner, especially after Worker losses or earlier underproduction. This originally aimed for 1 Worker per city plus a capital-area reserve. As a refinement, a one-city empire needs at most 1 land Worker until its capital has ever reached a tunable population (5 by default), avoiding early double-Worker production and the resulting halted growth; normal demand resumes after the capital matures or another city is founded. Ordinary Worker choices, cases where a blocked Settler choice falls back to a Worker, general unit selection, and Worker scrapping now use the same centralized target. Four BBAI test runs then completed exactly 1 early Worker per empire, with the only later automatic second Worker beginning when its capital reached population 5.
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6093+) Update: **AI Worker availability and land development substantially strengthened:** a closely matching under-improved-city symptom had already been recorded as high priority in a [Base AdvCiv test report](https://forums.civfanatics.com/threads/ai-city-placement-and-misc-suggestions.695343/page-7#post-16782814), where example 208 noted Shanghai was still almost unimproved in 325 AD despite being founded in 2320 BC. Much later, now in AdvCiv-SAS, Save file 459 then exposed both a separate AdvCiv-SAS Worker-demand fairly minor regression and a broader more impactful too-low standing minimum: Russia stayed at only 3 land Workers through turn 220 despite 6 cities; on turn 150, 39 of 62 worked plots were unimproved, rising to 58 of 91 by turn 200. To fix this, the AI now lets currently worked featureless land contribute Worker demand even though ordinary land improvement choice deliberately bypasses the legacy city best-build cache. More importantly, the primary-area standing minimum is XML-tunable through a percentage per city plus a **signed reserve**. The current 200% and -1 tuning gives a standing floor of 1 Worker at 1 city, 3 at 2 cities, 5 at 3, 7 at 4, etc.; positive reserve values add Workers, 0 leaves the percentage floor unchanged, and negative values reduce it while the final floor cannot fall below 1 Worker per city. This follow-up avoids the earlier 3-Worker one-city capital case that could delay the second Settler while retaining the stronger multi-city coverage. Demand, production ceilings and Worker-scrapping safeguards use the same minimum, while the one-city early-growth safeguard and demand-driven secondary areas remain. In the final save-file 459 branch, Russia had 9 Workers for 4 cities by turn 100 and 11 Workers for 5 cities by turn 150; only 7 of 54 worked plots were then unimproved and Moscow had all 16 worked plots improved. A later 100%-military test run also showed the intended early floor directly: Russia still had only 1 Worker at 1 city on turn 30, while Sitting Bull had 3 Workers after reaching 2 cities by turn 50. The historical Base AdvCiv symptom may have had additional causes; the exact `AI_getBestBuild` mismatch fixed here is SAS-specific. See [KI#198](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#198---fixedimproved-likely-base-advciv-workerland-development-underperformance-compounded-by-an-advciv-sas-worker-demand-regression-and-too-low-minimum-could-leave-mature-ai-cities-badly-unimproved).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6093+) Update: **AI primary landmasses now maintain roughly 2 Workers per city instead of roughly 1:** a [Base AdvCiv test report](https://forums.civfanatics.com/threads/ai-city-placement-and-misc-suggestions.695343/page-7#post-16782814) had already shown a long-established city remaining almost unimproved. Save file 459 later reproduced this severely in AdvCiv-SAS: Russia still had only 3 Workers for 6 cities on turn 220, with 58 of its 91 worked plots unimproved on turn 200. This exposed both a minor SAS Worker-demand mismatch and the old standing minimum of only 1 Worker per city plus 1 reserve being too low. To fix this, the AI now counts currently worked featureless land toward Worker demand, and its primary landmass keeps an XML-tunable floor of 2 Workers per city plus a signed reserve. Worker demand, production limits and scrapping share this floor. Initial testing with a +1 reserve greatly improved Russia's land but could delay its second Settler. Further save-file 459 comparisons showed that producing more Workers developed plots sooner but generally hurt population, production, military and expansion. Save file 463 confirmed the final reserve: raising only the 3+-city value from -1 to 0 improved more city-radius land on turn 100 (45.6% versus 39.5%), but left only 35 cities / 176 population / 167 production instead of 41 / 199 / 210; by turn 150 that improvement advantage had disappeared while -1 still led by 57 cities to 52 and 468 population to 415. The final independently tunable `-1 / -1 / -1` reserves provide 1 Worker at 1 city, 3 at 2 cities and 5 at 3 cities, then add 2 per additional city. A neglected jungle or new city may still reveal a local Worker-assignment issue, but globally producing another Worker proved worse. The early one-city safeguard and demand-driven secondary landmasses remain. See [KI#198](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#198---fixedimproved-likely-base-advciv-workerland-development-underperformance-compounded-by-an-advciv-sas-worker-demand-regression-and-too-low-minimum-could-leave-mature-ai-cities-badly-unimproved).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6060+) **Workers are no longer delivered to landmasses that already have enough:** in save file 450, BBAI logging showed a Worker accompanying the Settler to the island where Thapsus was founded, then remaining idle there for 93 turns because none of its plots could be improved. Our first attempt at this fix correctly left an unnecessary Worker on the large landmass (i.e. home) instead of sending it to the small unimprovable island, but exposed a separate problem: general Worker logic kept loading the Worker back onto the island-bound Settler transport, so our early fix had to unload the same Worker back onto the large landmass 14 times. After our second version correctly stopped that loop, testing exposed a third failure: a separate Worker-ferry routine still kept sending extra Workers to islands that already had enough, eventually crowding 5 Workers onto Sardis's two-tile landmass and 2 onto Ergili's three-tile landmass, where most had no useful work. To fix this, Settler transports now leave Workers beyond the destination's exact shortage in their original city, unload only the required number at the destination, and keep any excess aboard for another destination. Workers cannot repeatedly reboard the same transport after being left behind, and later Worker ferries now stop once the destination landmass has enough. These checks use current need and current Worker count, so delivery becomes eligible again if losses leave the landmass short or new work requires workers again. In the confirming run, the AI correctly left a Worker on its origin landmass because the destination needed 1 but already had 3. See [KI#192](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#192---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-island-worker-logistics-and-advciv-sas-production-safety-gap-could-strand-workers-and-other-land-civilians-where-they-had-no-use).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6061+) **Workers are brought home after finishing every useful task on a secondary landmass:** Base AdvCiv forced most landmasses to retain at least 1 Worker forever, even after a Worker had legitimately finished developing a small island and could contribute elsewhere. Secondary landmasses can now reach zero Worker demand when no useful task remains. Their finished Workers move to the coast and request pickup; one available Settler transport collects each Worker for reuse elsewhere, rather than several ships pursuing the same rescue. The primary landmass keeps its Worker reserve, and demand on a secondary landmass automatically returns if losses or new work require another Worker. In the confirming save file 450 run, Persia recovered all 5 Workers from its completed islands on the same turn, and 14 completed-area Worker boardings were recorded across the game. See [KI#192](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#192---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-island-worker-logistics-and-advciv-sas-production-safety-gap-could-strand-workers-and-other-land-civilians-where-they-had-no-use).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6062+) Note: the above zero-demand and pickup rule above does not apply to the Barbarian player, as Barbarian city improvement planning can report no local jobs until a Worker already exists, and Barbarians do not use the ordinary civilization system to transport and reassign finished Workers between separate landmasses; therefore each isolated Barbarian island keeps local Worker demand (else no Worker is built and Barbarian cities are not improved at all).

